WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:02.670
YUFENG GUO: Are you looking to
do some hyperparameter tuning

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.410
on your machine-learning model?

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.330
Or perhaps you have
other parameters

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.610
you'd like to be
optimizing in your life.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.940
Then you've got to check out
AI Platform Optimizer, one

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:14.966
of the newest products
from the Google AI Team.

00:00:14.966 --> 00:00:18.854
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.550
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:30.650
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:31.880
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.640
And on this episode,
we're going to dive

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:38.182
into the world of optimization
with AI Platform Optimizer.

00:00:38.182 --> 00:00:40.510
The AI Platform
Optimizer was built

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.883
to optimize parameters of
machine-learning models.

00:00:43.883 --> 00:00:45.550
So you can optimize
things like learning

00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:48.070
rate, batch size, and other
typical machine-learning

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:49.240
hyperparameters.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.180
This is the most common use
case for Optimizer today.

00:00:52.180 --> 00:00:53.240
But wait.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.560
There's more.

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:57.250
You can choose any
arbitrary values to tune.

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.080
In fact, it can optimize
any evaluable system.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.180
For example, you could
use it to determine

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:06.790
the most effective combination
of background color,

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.600
font size, and link color on
a news website's subscription

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.620
button.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:14.410
This would take A/B testing
to a whole new level.

00:01:14.410 --> 00:01:17.560
Or perhaps you need to find an
ideal buffer size and thread

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.810
count to minimize computing
resources for a job.

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:24.610
Or maybe you need to optimize a
cookie recipe, a bread recipe,

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:28.510
or a brownie recipe to produce
the most delicious version.

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:30.160
You can, of course,
also optimize

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.460
nonfood related things, though
I'm not sure why you'd want to.

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.390
Anyhow, let's talk about
how to use the AI Platform

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:40.390
Optimizer to optimize your
machine-learning model

00:01:40.390 --> 00:01:41.913
or other function.

00:01:41.913 --> 00:01:43.330
There are three
terms that we need

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:47.350
to understand-- study
configurations, studies,

00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:49.020
and trials.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.490
A study configuration
is the definition

00:01:51.490 --> 00:01:54.550
of the optimization problem
that you're trying to solve.

00:01:54.550 --> 00:01:57.130
It includes the result
you would like to optimize

00:01:57.130 --> 00:01:59.500
as well as the
parameters that affect

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:02.020
that result. Those are
basically the inputs that

00:02:02.020 --> 00:02:04.340
are being optimized.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:09.220
A study is the implementation
of a study configuration.

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:12.040
A study uses its
configuration's goal and input

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:16.330
parameters to conduct
experiments or trials.

00:02:16.330 --> 00:02:20.610
So then a trial is just a
specific set of input values

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.530
that produce some
measured outcome.

00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:26.740
AI Platform Optimizer
only suggests

00:02:26.740 --> 00:02:30.400
input values for each trial,
but it doesn't run your trials

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:31.330
for you.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:33.520
That is your task.

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.430
And a study will continue
until it reaches a preset limit

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:38.960
of trials or you end it.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.660
So this means
that, as a service,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.500
AI Platform Optimizer suggests
trials for you to run,

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.720
records the outcomes
of those trials,

00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.530
and then uses machine learning
to suggest future trials based

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:52.250
on those outcomes.

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.210
And you can continue the cycle
for as long as you please.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.930
Now, some of you may recall that
AI Platform Training already

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:02.320
has a built-in feature
called HyperTune, which takes

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:04.540
care of hyperparameter tuning.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:07.690
Under the hood, HyperTune
uses the same technology

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.640
as AI Platform Optimizer.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:12.970
But whereas HyperTune only
works as part of AI Platform

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:15.910
Training, Optimizer
is a generic system

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.700
that you can use to optimize
any system, any place.

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:22.550
This means that your model
could be training anywhere--

00:03:22.550 --> 00:03:24.610
your local machine,
a data center,

00:03:24.610 --> 00:03:27.920
or on another GCP service, like
Compute Engine or Kubernetes

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:28.810
Engine.

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.180
Anything that can
make a REST API call

00:03:31.180 --> 00:03:34.690
can use AI Platform Optimizer,
and that flexibility

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:37.190
is its true power.

